 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Sensing principle",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Each channel uses its MOSFET's own on-resistance as the current shunt: the comparator watches VDS between VD and VS and compares it with three thresholds. VTH2 (about -140 mV) turns the gate on, VTH1 (about -6 mV) turns it off, and VTH3 (a positive level, 2.5 V typ at VCC = 10 V) re-arms the channel. The result is a MOSFET that behaves like the secondary rectifier diode it replaces, switching close to zero current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "UVLO mode",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Below VCC UVLO the chip is in undervoltage lockout from any state: gates held inactive, most circuits unbiased, supply current ICC START (140 µA max). It leaves lockout only when VCC climbs past VCC ON (8.1 V typ); hysteresis is 0.5 V typ."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Normal mode",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Once VCC is above VCC ON, the gate outputs are enabled the first time the sensed VDS rises past VTH3. Waiting for VTH3 keeps a gate from firing late in a switching cycle, which would let reverse current flow during the minimum on time. With the drivers running, supply current is up to ICC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-on and minimum on time",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "When a rectifier starts to conduct, current first flows in the body diode and VDS goes well negative, past VTH2, and the gate is driven on. VDS then collapses to ID × RDS(on), usually with ringing that could trip the turn-off comparator; a fixed minimum on time (MOT, 850 ns typ) holds the gate on regardless. Because the MOT sets the shortest conduction time, it also caps the converter's switching frequency."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-off and blanking",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The gate stays on until the falling, sinusoidal rectifier current brings VDS up through VTH1; the slope there is gentle. After turn-off the residual current returns to the body diode and VDS steps negative, which could re-trigger VTH2, so VTH2 is blanked for tBLANK (17 µs typ) after VTH1 trips. The blanking ends early once VDS rises past VTH3, and the channel is ready for its next conduction interval."
  },
  {
   "topic": "MOT protection mode",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "At very light or no load the rectifier current can become discontinuous and shorter than the MOT. The MOSFET is then still on when current reverses from drain to source, which discharges Cout, stores energy in the transformer and sets VDS ringing after turn-off; that ringing can re-fire the gate and cause a chain of false triggers. The cycle-by-cycle MOT check sees a conduction interval shorter than the MOT and suppresses the next gate pulse. Comparator and MOT generator keep running in this mode, so the chip returns to normal operation by itself once conduction outlasts the MOT again. Dropping the pulses lowers standby loss and avoids voltage spikes from false triggering."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shoot-through protection",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Internal logic prevents GATE1 and GATE2 from being high at the same time."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The wide VCC range lets the chip run directly from the converter's output. VCC has no internal clamp; the gate outputs are clamped near 10.7 V, which limits gate-drive loss."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Turn-on delay tDon runs from VDS crossing VTH2 to the gate reaching 50 %; turn-off delay tDoff from VDS crossing VTH1 to the gate falling to 50 %. Rise and fall times are taken between 10 % and 90 % of the gate swing."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Place a ceramic bypass capacitor from VCC to ground right at the chip. Keep the drain-sense traces well routed, take VS1 and VS2 as Kelvin connections at each MOSFET's source pin rather than at pin 7, and keep each gate loop short and of small area. Series gate resistors are recommended, especially with paralleled MOSFETs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pin protection",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "VD1 and VD2 each reach the inside through a resistor RESD; from the pin to GND sit an ESD diode and a 200 V diode in series, the ESD diode bridged by a resistor. GATE1 and GATE2 each have ESD diodes to VCC and GND and a resistor to VCC."
  }
 ],
